PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. possibly 0

The definition of a symmetric game requires that player payoffs are invariant under any permutation of the players: if \(\sigma\) is a strategy profile, then the payoff to player \(i\) in \(\sigma\) equals the payoff to player \(j\) in the permuted profile \(\pi(\sigma)\) whenever \(\pi\) swaps \(i\) and \(j\).  

For \(n=2\) this reduces to the familiar condition \(u_1(x,y)=u_2(y,x)\).  Choosing payoffs \(u_1(1,1)=2\), \(u_1(1,2)=0\), \(u_1(2,1)=3\), \(u_1(2,2)=1\) yields a symmetric game whose four pure profiles each fail the Nash condition:
- both play 1: player 1 gains by deviating;
- both play 2: player 2 gains by deviating;
- (1,2): player 1 gains by deviating;
- (2,1): player 2 gains by deviating.

Thus the minimum can be zero.  The same construction extends immediately to \(n>2\) by letting all but two players play a fixed action and embedding the two-player counter-example in the remaining coordinates; symmetry is preserved and no new pure equilibria are created.  Consequently the answer is A for every \(n\).

(The other options are ruled out because the construction shows that zero is attainable, so any claimed positive lower bound is false.)

